   * Proposal Baseline (Section 3.3): The binary baseline rule (v > 0) causes severe alarm fatigue by falsely alerting on harmless minor budget variances and normal seasonal fluctuations (such as labour or machinery drifting 1–8% within safe limits), while failing to distinguish between early advisory warnings and critical emergencies.
   * Extra Baseline (Flat 10%): The flat 10% baseline rule misses critical early intervention windows for volatile inputs (such as seed, fertilizer, and fuel drifting between 7% and 10%), while simultaneously over-escalating routine 11–15% stable operational variances into emergency alarms.
   * Tiered Mode: Tiered mode successfully mitigates false alarms and early misses by calibrating tolerances against category volatility, but relies on static category classifications and currently treats severe agricultural under-spending as normal.

